// EXPECT: [E126]
// A mutable borrow cannot be STORED IN A LOCAL. (Was `direct_var_borrow_fail.ln` asserting
// [E004]; the construct it used has never compiled, so what it locked was unreachable.)
//
// ADJUDICATED 2026-09-15 while writing local/thesis/chapters/02-borrow.tex. `var ref = var
// data.value` emitted `int32_t ref = &(data.value);` — gcc only WARNS on that, so a truncated
// address was stored in an int and no gate saw it. The whole-struct form emitted
// `P r = &(a);`, a hard gcc error (D-30).
//
// E126 already rejected the same construct as a struct FIELD. Extending it to locals makes the
// restriction uniform, which is the point: the borrow checker is call-site-scoped BECAUSE a
// reference cannot be stored. It is also exactly the axiom of mutable value semantics —
// references live only for the call that creates them, stored neither in variables nor in
// object fields.

type Data {
    value i32
}

proc main() i32 {
    var data Data
    data.value = 42
    var ref = var data.value
    return 0
}
